.TH MKSTEMP 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
mkstemp \- create and open an exclusive temporary file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>
int mkstemp(char *template);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
template must be a writable string ending in six X characters. mkstemp
replaces them with a candidate suffix and opens with O_RDWR, O_CREAT and
O_EXCL, requesting mode 0600. Helios server access matrices determine actual
access rights. Exclusive creation depends on the target server supporting it.
.PP
On success the modified template names the file and the caller owns its
descriptor. Close the descriptor and unlink the file when finished.
The function retries name collisions up to 256 times. Suffixes are generated
from time, task ID and a counter; they are not random tokens.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int temporary_work(void)
{
    char name[] = "/helios/tmp/workXXXXXX";
    int fd = mkstemp(name);
    if (fd < 0) return -1;
    /* Read and write through fd here. */
    close(fd);
    return unlink(name);
}
.EE
.SH RETURN VALUE
An open descriptor, or -1 with errno set. EINVAL indicates an invalid template;
EEXIST indicates exhausted collision retries. Other open errors propagate.
.SH SEE ALSO
glob(3), helios-unix(7)
